{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"edits\":[{\"path\":\"history/0/text\",\"value_json\":\"Early forms are reported with 女 and 子 in variable positions; side-switched forms are treated as variants, not chronological ancestors. Some Warring States bamboo forms are reported with 丑 in place of 女. The sources do not establish a complete sequence of graphic change.\"},{\"path\":\"history/1/text\",\"value_json\":\"The selected oracle- and bronze-script illustrations are modern vector redrawings, not artifact photographs; their appearance does not establish a sequence of change.\"},{\"path\":\"history/1/evidence_ids\",\"value_json\":\"[\\\"ref035\\\",\\\"ref036\\\"]\"},{\"path\":\"history/2/text\",\"value_json\":\"In the Shang name 婦好, a separate proposal treats 好 as a possible homograph: 子 may supply the sound and represent a lineage name. This name analysis does not establish a phonetic role for 子 in ordinary 好.\"},{\"path\":\"history/3/text\",\"value_json\":\"A bronze inscription is reported to use 好 as a phonetic loan for 孝. In a phonetic loan, a sign is reused for a different word because their spoken forms sound alike or similar; the account supplies no sound comparison, so the proposed match remains uncertain.\"},{\"path\":\"history/3/evidence_ids\",\"value_json\":\"[\\\"ref023\\\",\\\"ref045\\\",\\\"ref048\\\"]\"}]}"}}
